    <section class="af__step" :class="isOn(0) ? 'is-on' : ''">
      <div class="af__head">
        <h2>Event details</h2>
        <p>The name, when it happens and where. Only the title and the start are required —
          everything else can wait, and an event saved as a draft is not visible to anybody.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="row">
        <label>Title <span class="af-req" title="Required">*</span>
          <input type="text" name="title" value="{{ row.title|default('') }}" required></label>
        <label>Slug <span class="af-opt">blank = made from the title</span>
          <input type="text" name="slug" value="{{ row.slug|default('') }}" placeholder="auto"></label>

        {# ── WHEN THE MIGRATION HAS NOT RUN ────────────────────────────────
           The house convention on this form is to HIDE a field whose column does not
           exist yet — an operator uploads the zip and runs /__setup/migrate as two
           separate acts, and a form that posts an unknown column 500s in between.

           Hiding it silently is the wrong half of that convention here. Every other
           hidden field on this page is a refinement of something already visible; this
           one is the ONLY way to link an award to a ceremony, and the whole invitation
           run reads from it. Gone with no explanation, it does not read as "not migrated
           yet", it reads as "that feature does not exist" — which is what it read as.
           So the control goes, and the reason stays. #}
        {# The DIAGNOSIS, not a fixed sentence. This block used to say "run the migration
           once — /__setup/migrate" whatever the actual state was, which is the exact
           instruction that cannot work when the step is recorded as applied and the table
           is absent: the endpoint skips it forever. It went on saying it after the
           operator had run it, repeatedly, so the feature read as one that had never been
           built. EventInvites::linkBlocker() decides which of the two faults it is. #}
        {% if programme_link %}
        <div style="grid-column:1/-1;background:#fdf6e3;border:1px solid #f3b416;border-radius:11px;padding:12px 14px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.7;color:#10292C">
          <strong style="display:block;margin-bottom:2px">Awards cannot be linked to this event yet.</strong>
          {{ programme_link.what }} {{ programme_link.fix }}
          {% if programme_link.rerun and row.id is defined %}
            {# formaction, NOT a nested <form>. A <form> inside a <form> is DELETED by the
               parser — not nested, dropped — and this button would then have run the event
               save instead. Three of those have shipped here; the house convention is a
               formaction on the submit button and this is it.

               `?back=form` so the redirect lands where the button was pressed. Without it
               an operator on the event form is thrown to the invitation screen, which is
               not where they were and not what they were doing. #}
            <div style="margin-top:10px">
              <button class="ad-btn" type="submit"
                      formaction="/admin/events/{{ row.id }}/invites/repair?back=form"
                      formnovalidate>Create the awards link now</button>
              <span style="margin-left:8px;color:var(--cn-ink-3)">Applies
                <code>{{ programme_link.rerun }}</code> again. Safe to press twice.</span>
            </div>
          {% endif %}
        </div>
        {% endif %}
        {% if programme_link_ready %}
        {# CHECKBOXES, not a multi-select. A <select multiple> on a phone is a scrolling
           list where ctrl-click is the only way to add a second item, and this is the
           control that decides whose shortlist gets invited — so it is four visible
           checkboxes an operator can read and tick.

           A hidden empty field first, so CLEARING every box actually clears the set:
           unchecked boxes send nothing at all, and a save with none ticked would otherwise
           look identical to a save from a form that never had the field. #}
        {# A fieldset with a legend, not a label over a div: these are several controls
           answering one question, which is exactly what a group label is for — a screen
           reader reading the fourth checkbox otherwise has no idea what the four are. #}
        <fieldset style="grid-column:1/-1;border:0;padding:0;margin:0;min-width:0">
          <legend style="display:block;font-weight:600;font-size:13px;color:var(--cn-ink);margin-bottom:6px;padding:0">
            Awards presented at this event
            <small style="display:block;font-weight:400;color:var(--cn-ink-3)">the invitation run draws its guests of honour from these — leave all unticked when the event is not a ceremony</small>
          </legend>
          {# An empty value first, so CLEARING every box actually clears the set: unchecked
             boxes send nothing at all, and a save with none ticked would otherwise be
             indistinguishable from a save posted by a form that never had the field. #}
          <input type="hidden" name="programme_ids[]" value="">
          <div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px 18px">
            {% for p in programmes %}
              <label style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;font-weight:400;font-size:14px;min-height:44px;margin:0">
                <input type="checkbox" name="programme_ids[]" value="{{ p.id }}"
                       {% if p.id in programme_ids %}checked{% endif %}>
                {{ p.title }}
              </label>
            {% endfor %}
          </div>
        </fieldset>
        {% endif %}
      </div>
      {# ── THE EVENT'S OWN CLOCK ─────────────────────────────────────────────
         The zone sits WITH the times rather than in a settings screen, and it is read
         back into the two fields beside it: an event is a room in a city at a wall-clock
         time, and the only hour that matters is the one on the clock in that room.

         `|event_input` rather than `|when_input`, so a Nairobi gala is shown and typed
         in Nairobi. Blank means the platform's own zone, which is what every event
         before this field had — so nothing an organiser has already saved moves. #}
      <div class="row">
        <label>Starts <span class="af-req" title="Required">*</span>
          <input type="datetime-local" name="event_date" value="{{ event_input(row, row.event_date|default('')) }}" required step="1"></label>
        <label>Ends <span class="af-opt">optional</span>
          <input type="datetime-local" name="end_date" value="{{ event_input(row, row.end_date|default('')) }}" step="1"></label>
      </div>
      <label for="evTz">Timezone <span class="af-opt">the clock at the venue — leave as the default unless the event is somewhere else</span>
        <select name="timezone" id="evTz">
          <option value="">Platform default — {{ tz_abbr() }}</option>
          {% for z in tz_choices %}
            <option value="{{ z }}"{{ (row.timezone|default('')) == z ? ' selected' : '' }}>{{ z }}</option>
          {% endfor %}
        </select>
      </label>
      <div class="row">
        <label>Town or city <span class="af-opt">optional</span>
          <input type="text" name="location" value="{{ row.location|default('') }}" placeholder="Lagos · Nigeria"></label>
        <label>Venue <span class="af-opt">optional</span>
          <input type="text" name="venue" value="{{ row.venue|default('') }}" placeholder="Eko Hotel"></label>
      </div>
      <label>Tagline <span class="af-opt">one line, shown under the title</span>
        <input type="text" name="tagline" value="{{ row.tagline|default('') }}" maxlength="255"></label>
      <label>Description <span class="af-opt">optional</span>
        <textarea name="description" rows="5">{{ row.description|default('') }}</textarea></label>

      <div class="event-editor__cover">
        <h3>Event cover</h3>
        {% if row.cover_image|default('') %}<img src="{{ row.cover_image starts with 'http' ? row.cover_image : '/' ~ row.cover_image|trim('/') }}" alt="Current event cover" loading="lazy" width="240" height="160">{% endif %}
        {% include 'partials/photo-upload.twig' with {id:'event-cover', name:'cover_upload', label:'Choose an event cover', min_side:300, help:'A landscape image works best. At least 300 pixels on each side.'} only %}
        <details class="event-editor__optional"><summary>Use an existing image address</summary>
          <label>Image URL or upload path<input type="text" name="cover_image" value="{{ row.cover_image|default('') }}" placeholder="https://… or uploads/events/…"></label>
          <p class="af-note">A newly uploaded cover replaces this image when you save.</p>
        </details>
      </div>
      <div class="row">
        <label>Map link <span class="af-opt">optional</span>
          <input type="url" name="map_embed" value="{{ row.map_embed|default('') }}" placeholder="https://maps.google.com/…"></label>
        <label>External details page <span class="af-opt">optional</span>
          <input type="text" name="rsvp_url" value="{{ row.rsvp_url|default('') }}" placeholder="/partner or https://…"></label>
      </div>
    </section>
